\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {H}{\ignorespaces Diversification and the systematic floor (illustrative, \emph  {beyond the book}): an equal-weighted portfolio of $n$ securities, each with $\sigma =30\%$ and pairwise $\rho =0.25$. Then $\sigma _V^2=\sigma ^2/n+(1-1/n)\rho \sigma ^2$. The diversifiable part goes to zero as $n$ grows; the systematic floor $\sqrt  \rho \,\sigma $ stays.}}{16}{figure.caption.31}\protected@file@percent }
